exact_canonical_statement_tex:
SA06:C.3bm: C.3bm. The universal exceptional-surface reduction in positive characteristic, dimension three (Proved reduction).. Let $k$ be any algebraically closed field of characteristic $p>0$. Let $R$ be the strict henselization at a closed $k$-point of a normal local ring essentially of finite type over $k$. Suppose that $\dim R=3$ and that \[ X=\operatorname{Spec}R\setminus\{\mathfrak m_R\} \] is regular. There exists a regular projective modification $f:Z\to\operatorname{Spec}R$, an isomorphism over $X$, whose full reduced closed fibre $E=\bigcup_{i=1}^c E_i$ is an SNC divisor and which satisfies the hypotheses of EL and EP. In particular, with \[ S(E)=F_\gamma^2K_0(E)_{\mathbf Q},\qquad J_K(E)=\left\langle\beta_t-\beta_o: \begin{array}{l} P/k\text{ smooth connected projective curve},\\ o,t\in P(k),\quad\beta\in F_\gamma^2K_0(E\times P)_{\mathbf Q} \end{array}\right\rangle, \] and with $\theta:\mathrm{CH}_1(E)_{\mathbf Q}\to S(E)$ the exceptional pushforward of EP, there is an isomorphism \begin{equation} B^2(X)_{\mathbf Q}\simeq \frac{S(E)}{J_K(E)+\theta\mathrm{CH}_1(E)_{\mathbf Q}}. \tag{C.3bm.1} \end{equation} Thus the exceptional-family classes defining the relation subgroup are unrestricted actual perfect classes on $E\times P$: no extension of that class to $Z\times P$, and no vanishing of its fixed-family negative-$K$ boundary, is required. There is also an explicit degree-zero form of the remaining gate. Define \[ \deg_E:S(E)\longrightarrow\mathbf Q^c, \qquad a\longmapsto \bigl(\deg\operatorname{ch}_2(a|_{E_i})\bigr)_i, \qquad S_0(E)=\ker(\deg_E). \] Then $J_K(E)\subset S_0(E)$, and \begin{equation} B^2(X)_{\mathbf Q}\simeq \frac{S_0(E)}{J_K(E)+ \bigl(S_0(E)\cap\theta\mathrm{CH}_1(E)_{\mathbf Q}\bigr)}. \tag{C.3bm.2} \end{equation} The theorem holds for every prime, including $2$ and $3$. It removes the ambient endpoint-lifting condition from this entire algebraizable threefold branch. It does not assert that the quotient in (C.3bm.2) vanishes or is finite-dimensional, and makes no claim in characteristic zero.

SA06:C.3bo: C.3bo. Weighted exceptional intersections remove the component Albanese coordinates (Proved reduction).. Retain the data and notation of C.3bm. Thus $k$ is algebraically closed of characteristic $p>0$, $Z$ is the regular projective threefold modification, $E=\bigcup_iE_i$ is its full SNC divisor, and $D=\sum_i a_iE_i$ has $a_i>0$ and $\mathcal O_E(-D)$ ample. Put $A_i=\operatorname{Pic}^0_{E_i/k,\mathrm{red}}$, $B_i=\operatorname{Alb}(E_i)=A_i^\vee$, and $A=\prod_iA_i$. The canonical Albanese class of a degree-zero cycle is independent of a base point. Define \begin{align} \alpha_E:S_0(E)&\longrightarrow\bigoplus_iB_i(k)\otimes\mathbf Q, \notag\\ s&\longmapsto \bigl(\operatorname{alb}_{E_i}(\operatorname{ch}_2(s|_{E_i}))\bigr)_i, &S_{00}(E)&=\ker(\alpha_E). \tag{AM.1} \end{align} Write $U=\theta(\bigoplus_i A_i(k))_{\mathbf Q}$, using the divisor-cycle maps $A_i(k)\to\mathrm{CH}_1(E_i)$. Then \begin{equation} U\subset J_K(E),\qquad \alpha_E(U)=\bigoplus_iB_i(k)\otimes\mathbf Q. \tag{AM.2} \end{equation} Consequently inclusion induces \begin{align} \frac{S_0(E)}{J_K(E)} &\simeq\frac{S_{00}(E)}{J_K(E)\cap S_{00}(E)}, \tag{AM.3}\\ B^2(X)_{\mathbf Q} &\simeq \frac{S_{00}(E)} {(J_K(E)\cap S_{00}(E))+ (\theta\mathrm{CH}_1(E)_{\mathbf Q}\cap S_{00}(E))}. \tag{AM.4} \end{align} The component Albanese kernels and perfect-class compatibility along SNC intersections remain in (AM.4). No vanishing is asserted.

SA06:C.3bt: C.3bt. Proper edge families (Proved).. Let $k$ be algebraically closed of characteristic $p>0$. Let $E=\bigcup E_i$ be a projective SNC exceptional surface in a regular threefold, with connected double curves $C_e$ and individually indexed triple points. Assume that $D=\sum a_iE_i$, $a_i>0$, satisfies $\mathcal O_E(-D)$ ample. Put $S(E)=F_\gamma^2K_0(E)_{\mathbf Q}$, and let $J_K(E)$ be the actual fixed smooth-proper-curve endpoint relation group of EP. Then \begin{equation} \ker\left(S(E)\longrightarrow \bigoplus_i\operatorname{CH}_0(E_i)_{\mathbf Q}\right) \subseteq J_K(E). \tag{VE.1} \end{equation} There is no hypothesis on second dual cohomology, triple-point Picard periods, or joint Picard restriction. In the exact resolved threefold scope of C.3bm this removes the edge and triple-point kernel. After C.3bo the remaining restrictions lie in the component Albanese kernels, with their induced proper-family and exceptional relations. If all component Albanese kernels vanish rationally, the ordinary rational top quotient of the puncture is zero. The general component kernel quotient is not computed here. The new input is the following actual-family lemma. Its marked restrictions are literally zero in $K_1$, not merely norm-zero. \emph{Marked edge-family lemma.} Let $C_1,\ldots,C_s$ be smooth connected proper curves over $k$, with base points $c_e$ and finitely many marked points on each. For $L_e\in\operatorname{Pic}^0(C_e)(k)$ and $u\in k^*$ there are a smooth connected proper curve $P$, points $o,t\in P(k)$, and actual classes $\xi_e\in K_1(C_e\times P)$ such that \begin{align} \xi_e|_{C_e\times\{o\}}&=0,& \xi_e|_{C_e\times\{t\}}&=u([L_e]-1),\nonumber\\ \xi_e|_{\{q\}\times P}&=0 &&\text{for every marked point }q. \tag{VE.2} \end{align} The construction on smooth products is integral; the singular-scheme application below uses rational coefficients. \emph{1. A proper base and normalized divisors.} If $u=1$ or every $L_e$ is trivial, take zero classes. Otherwise choose a smooth connected projective curve $A\subset\prod_e\operatorname{Pic}^0(C_e)$ through $0$ and the nonzero tuple $(L_e)_e$. In dimension one use the whole abelian variety; otherwise successive sufficiently high complete intersections with prescribed points and first jets give such a curve in every characteristic. The smoothness incidence estimate and connectedness by duality and Serre vanishing are the same ones used in C.3bq \citep[Lemma 33.47.3 and proof]{D_StacksBertiniUniformHigher}. Denote its two points by $a_0,a_1$. The normalized Poincare bundles $\mathcal M_e$ on $C_e\times A$ are trivialized along $c_e\times A$ and $C_e\times\{a_0\}$, and have fibres $L_e$ at $a_1$. For every marked point $q$, put $N_{e,q}=\mathcal M_e|_{\{q\}\times A}$, with its fixed trivialization at $a_0$. Choose a rational section $r_{e,q}$ having value $1$ in that trivialization at $a_0$, and divisor $B_{e,q}=\operatorname{div}(r_{e,q})$ disjoint from $a_0,a_1$. To do this, twist by a large effective divisor supported away from the two points. Vanishing of $H^1$ after subtracting both points makes evaluation onto, so prescribe value $1$ at $a_0$ and a nonzero value at $a_1$, then divide by the twisting section. Only finitely many divisors are involved. \emph{2. Interpolate the covering function at their supports.} Let $F$ be the union of all their supports. Choose a rational function $h$ on $A$, with its only pole at $a_0$, satisfying \begin{equation} h(a_1)=\frac{(u-1)^2}{u},\qquad h(x)=0\quad(x\in F), \tag{VE.3} \end{equation} and having at least one simple zero away from $a_0,a_1$. For the finite prescribed jet scheme $T$, choose $n$ large enough that \[ H^1(A,\mathcal O_A(na_0)(-T))=0. \] This makes all the conditions compatible. A nonzero leading polar coefficient at $a_0$ can be prescribed too. One may impose simple zeros at every point of $F$ and at one further point. This is the curve Riemann--Roch evaluation argument; there is no bound on the characteristic. The value at $a_1$ is nonzero because $u\ne1$. Normalize the double cover with function-field equation \begin{equation} (g-1)^2=h g,\qquad g^2-(h+2)g+1=0. \tag{VE.4} \end{equation} In odd characteristic its discriminant $h(h+4)$ has odd valuation at a simple zero of $h$, proving irreducibility and separability. In characteristic two, $y=(g+1)/h$ satisfies $y^2+y=1/h$. The right side has a simple pole, whereas any pole of $z^2+z$ has even order, so this equation is again irreducible and separable. Thus the normalization $f:P\to A$ is a finite separable degree-two map of smooth connected proper curves. Both $g$ and $g^{-1}$ are integral wherever $h$ is regular. Every zero or pole of $g$ therefore lies above $a_0$. Conversely $g+g^{-1}=h+2$ shows that $g$ is not a unit at any point above that pole. Write $B=f^{-1}(a_0)_{\rm red}$. Choose $o\in B$ and a point $t$ above $a_1$ with $g(t)=u$. Such a point exists by (VE.4), including when it is ramified. For every point $y$ above $F$, the same equation gives \begin{equation} g(y)=1. \tag{VE.5} \end{equation} This holds also in characteristic two and with ramification. \emph{3. An actual Cech $K_2$ class across zeros and poles.} Fix an edge and write $X=C_e\times P$, $\mathcal M=(1\times f)^*\mathcal M_e$ and $V=C_e\times B$. The bundle has its specified trivialization on $V$. Choose a finite Zariski cover trivializing $\mathcal M$, using local frames lifting that trivialization wherever an open meets $V$. Such frames exist by locally lifting a basis of the invertible module modulo the ideal of $V$ and shrinking. Let $b_{ij}$ be the transition units, with the convention that their Cech class is $c_1(\mathcal M)$. Off $V$ take the symbols $\{g,b_{ij}\}$ in the Zariski sheaf $\mathcal K_2$ of Quillen $K$-groups. Near a point above $a_0$, choose a parameter $z$ on $P$ and write $g=z^m v$, $b_{ij}=1+z b'_{ij}$, where $v,b_{ij}$ are units and $m$ may be negative. In additive notation the extension is \begin{equation} m\langle z,-b'_{ij}\rangle+\{v,1+z b'_{ij}\}. \tag{VE.6} \end{equation} Indeed the Dennis--Stein convention is $\langle r,s\rangle=\{r,1-rs\}$ when $r$ is invertible \citep[Chapter III, Definition 5.11 and identities D1--D3, pp.~43--44]{frWeibelKbookIII}. Regular local Gersten injectivity identifies the sheaf $\mathcal K_2$ with its unramified subsheaf in the function-field group \citep[Section 7, Theorem 5.11 and the preceding Gersten complex] {rootQuillen1973}. Thus the extensions glue uniquely and satisfy the Cech cocycle identity, since they do so in the function field. Changing compatible local frames gives a Cech coboundary: their transition units are also $1$ modulo $z$, and the same formula extends the corresponding symbols. We obtain \begin{equation} c_e\in H^1(X,\mathcal K_2). \tag{VE.7} \end{equation} This uses regular local sheaf injectivity, not injectivity of an arbitrary global affine $K_2$ group into its function field. Functoriality of the displayed symbols gives the horizontal endpoint and vertical marked-point restrictions used below. At a zero or pole endpoint, (VE.6) specializes to $m\langle0,-b'_{ij}|_{z=0}\rangle+\{v|_{z=0},1\}=0$; the first zero follows directly from the defining Dennis--Stein word. Hence $c_e|_o=0$. At $t$ it is the ordinary cup $u\,c_1(L_e)\in H^1(C_e,\mathcal K_2)$. \emph{4. Marked restrictions, including the boundary scalar.} At a marked $q$, the restricted relative-trivialized bundle is $f^*N_{e,q}$. The section $f^*r_{e,q}$ has value $1$ at every point of $B$, and divisor \[ \sum_{x\in\operatorname{supp}B_{e,q}}\ \sum_{y\mid x} e(y/x)n_x[y], \qquad B_{e,q}=\sum_x n_x[x]. \] Use this section as frame away from its divisor. It is compatible with the specified boundary trivialization. All remaining transition calculations occur where $g$ is a unit. The Gersten divisor formula for the restricted cup is consequently \begin{equation} \sum_x\sum_{y\mid x} e(y/x)n_x\,g(y)[\mathcal O_y] \quad\text{in }H^1(P,\mathcal K_2). \tag{VE.8} \end{equation} A unit is here written additively as its $K_1(k)$ class. This is also the proper point-pushforward projection formula \citep[Section 7, Propositions 2.10 and 3.2]{rootQuillen1973}. No boundary point-unit term is omitted: the comparison frame has value $1$ at the boundary, so its comparison symbols extend by (VE.6), with zero specializations. This explains the normalization of the rational sections in Step~1. Every $g(y)$ in (VE.8) equals $1$ by (VE.5), hence is zero in $K_1(k)$. Ramification multiplicities, including wild ones in characteristic two, make no difference. Every marked restriction of $c_e$ is literally zero. No norm map is used. \emph{5. Lift to the actual $K_1$ group by sheaf descent.} For a noetherian finite-dimensional scheme there is the natural strongly convergent descent spectral sequence \begin{equation} E_2^{a,b}=H^a(X,\mathcal K_b)\Longrightarrow K_{b-a}(X), \qquad d_r:(a,b)\longmapsto(a+r,b+r-1). \tag{VE.9} \end{equation} Use \citet[Theorem 10.3 and proof, pp.~383--386; Appendix D.4--D.5 and proofs, pp.~424--426]{D_ThomasonTrobaugh1990}. The filtration is the sheaf hypercohomology/Postnikov filtration, not an assertion about pullback preserving codimension of supports. Derived pullback of perfect complexes induces the map of $K$-presheaves \citep[Sections 3.14--3.15, pp.~317--319]{D_ThomasonTrobaugh1990}. The natural augmentation and functorial Postnikov construction give the compatible descent-filtration maps for the horizontal and vertical regular Cartier curves at issue. As $\dim X=2$, no differential of order $r\ge2$ can enter or leave $(a,b)=(1,2)$: its source would have negative cohomological degree or its target would have degree at least three. Hence \begin{equation} F^1_{\rm des}K_1(X)/F^2_{\rm des}K_1(X) \simeq H^1(X,\mathcal K_2). \tag{VE.10} \end{equation} Choose a lift $\xi_e\in F^1_{\rm des}K_1(X)$ of $c_e$. For a regular curve the same descent filtration has $F^2_{\rm des}=0$ and $F^1_{\rm des}K_1\simeq H^1(\mathcal K_2)$. Naturality makes the required restrictions of $\xi_e$ independent of its choice and equal to the sheaf classes computed above. The possible degree-two surface filtration ambiguity restricts to zero on these Cartier curves, while choosing $F^1_{\rm des}$ excludes a determinant/unit term. The ordinary cup on a smooth curve sends $u\,c_1(L_e)$ to $u([L_e]-1)$. Thus (VE.2) holds for actual classes on the fixed proper products. No formal or affine-only family has been substituted. \input{agents/real_weil/C3_unconditional_edge_totalization.tex}
